Drop the length modifier when hexdump pads a conversion with spaces.
Past the end of the input, bpad() turns a conversion into %s and prints
an empty string to fill its field width, but it left any length
modifier in place, so %lld, and every %_a offset, which rewrite() turns
into %lld, %llo or %llx, became %lls. That is not a valid conversion,
and a C library may take it as a wide string, as it does %ls, and read
past the empty string. Remove the l characters before the conversion
character as well.

---
 libbb/dump.c |    6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

--- a/libbb/dump.c
+++ b/libbb/dump.c
@@ -536,6 +536,12 @@
 	 */
 	pr->flags = F_BPAD;
 	*pr->cchar = 's';
+	/* and any length modifier: "%lls" is not a plain string */
+	for (p1 = pr->cchar; p1[-1] == 'l'; p1--)
+		if (pr->nospace)
+			pr->nospace--;
+	overlapping_strcpy(p1, pr->cchar);
+	pr->cchar = p1;
 	for (p1 = pr->fmt; *p1 != '%'; ++p1)
 		continue;
 	for (p2 = ++p1; *p1 && strchr(" -0+#", *p1); ++p1)
